  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThrough Wednesday: Our CWA will remain on the eastern periphery of a \nlarge 596dam high while a couple disturbances top the ridge and dive \ndown across our region within the resulting northwest flow aloft. \nConvection associated with one such disturbance was noted on local \nradars over the western half of the CWA this afternoon. This \nactivity will dissipate as it moves south of our CWA this evening \nbut convection associated with an additional disturbance is progged \nto drop into our northeast zones later tonight. Convection Wednesday \nis expected to be more focused across the eastern half of our CWA. \nThis will allow our western half to warm up into the mid and upper \n90s. The 12Z Tue JAN sounding had a PWAT of 2.10inches and PWATs wl \nremain around two inches through Wednesday. Thus, heat stress is \nexpected to increase over the whole CWA Wednesday with the highest \nheat indices over the western half of our CWA. In the northeast \nwhere peak heat index values will ne around 105F a \"Limited\" threat \narea for dangerous heat will be advertised. Elsewhere an \"Elevated\" \nthreat for dangerous heat will be advertised for heat indices \nbetween 105-110F. Depending on how the morning convection plays out \nin the east Wednesday, the late afternoon convection may become \nstrong to severe with damaging wind gusts being the main threat.\n/22/\n\nWednesday Night through next Monday...The ongoing northwest flow \npattern across the region will persist through the end of the work \nweek.  This will continue to provide decent rain chances across the \nforecast area as disturbances embedded in this flow continue to move \nacross the region.  That being said, the main change in the forecast \npackage for this afternoon is that confidence has increased to \nadvertise both a \"Slight Risk\" (level 2 of 5) and \"Marginal Risk\" \n(level 1 of 5) for severe storms across portions of the forecast \narea on Wednesday, mainly during the Wednesday evening to early \nThursday morning timeframe.\n\nDuring this time, a MCV moving through the ArkLaTex Wednesday \nafternoon, where temperatures will hover around 100 degrees and \nample instability will exist, is progged to spark showers and some \nrobust thunderstorms across that area.  Timing of this MCV and the \nconvection associated with it moving through the CWA isn't ideal, \ni.e. during peak heating.  However, there will be enough instability \npresent, particularly Wednesday evening, along with some weak but \noverall modest flow, that some severe storms will be possible across \nthe area as the MCV moves through, again mainly Wednesday evening \ninto early Thursday morning. Damaging wind gusts up to 60 mph will \nbe the primary concern with the most intense storms, particularly \nacross far northwest portions of the Delta region where a \"Slight \nRisk\" for severe storms resides. However, frequent lightning and \nheavy downpours can be expected. As the MCV, and a likely outflow \nboundary, moves further east southeast across the CWA into early \nThursday morning, convection will weaken as instability likewise \nweakens. Chances for showers and storms will remain in the forecast \nThursday afternoon and again on Friday, especially across locations \nalong and southeast of the Natchez Trace Parkway.\n\nOver the weekend and into early next week, high pressure aloft will \nbegin to re-assert itself across the Lower Mississippi River Valley. \nThis will result in lessening rain chances across the forecast area. \nUnfortunately, the trade-off will again be increasing heat.  By next \nMonday, some need for at least a \"Limited\" threat of increased heat \nstress may exist across portions of the forecast area. /19/",
